diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce the reported outage, and the OSPF route for VLAN 30 (172.16.30.0/24) is present across the core and distribution. Live reachability from the affected source works: PC1 (10.5.0.1) successfully pings hosts in VLAN 30 (e.g., 172.16.30.12 and .13) with 0% loss and ~2–10 ms RTT. 

Core/control-plane verification shows the route exists and is being used:
- router7 (core) has OSPF O>* 172.16.30.0/24 via 172.30.1.1 on eth4.
- router10 and router11 also have OSPF entries to 172.16.30.0/24 via their upstreams.
- router16 (Campus 1 aggregation) has O>* 172.16.30.0/24 via 10.2.2.2 (bond0), confirming end-to-end advertisement.

Mechanism: With the OSPF route in place, traffic from 10.5.0.0/24 to 172.16.30.0/24 forwards normally through the core (router7 → 172.30.1.1 path), which matches the successful ping tests. Localization: OSPF on router7 eth4 toward 172.30.1.1 and upstream routers is healthy.

Root cause: No fault found; the alleged “missing OSPF route for 172.16.30.0/24” is not occurring, and connectivity is currently normal.

Corrective fix: No changes required. Recommend closing the ticket as not reproducible. If the user observes a recurrence, collect timestamped traceroute and “show ip route 172.16.30.0/24” from the core at that moment to investigate any transient conditions.
